<BaseLayout title="AI Literacy · Hearth & Code" description="A review-required Hearth & Code position on AI literacy, public governance, and evidence boundaries.">
  <StudioNavigation current="philosophy" disposition="Philosophy, AI literacy, and public governance" />
<main class="ec-page" id="page-main">
  <article class="ec-essay ec-policy-page">
    <header class="ec-essay-header">
      <p class="ec-eyebrow">Philosophy · AI Literacy</p>
      <h1>AI Literacy as a Developing Practice</h1>
      <p class="ec-subtitle">Understand the system. Check the claim. Keep the decision visible.</p>
      <dl class="ec-meta" aria-label="Article details">
        <div><dt>Status</dt><dd class="ec-status-review">Local revision candidate · not republished</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Revision date</dt><dd><time datetime="2026-10-01">2026-10-01</time></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Reading time</dt><dd>About 6 minutes</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="ec-review-note">This local draft revises a page previously published on 2026-09-23. The earlier publication record remains historical; this revision has not been approved or republished.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="ec-section">
      <h2>Position</h2>
      <p>AI literacy is not a badge or a one-time checklist. It is the developing ability to understand enough about a system and its use to ask what it can do, where its limits matter, how to check its output, and who remains responsible for the decision.</p>
      <p>Hearth &amp; Code treats that as both a personal practice and a governance question. People need usable ways to question AI; providers and deployers also shape the systems, contexts, training, and safeguards around use. This is our working position, not a validated literacy curriculum or a claim of compliance with a standard.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="ec-section">
      <h2>What public frameworks say</h2>
      <p>The sources describe different audiences and responsibilities, so they should not be collapsed into one universal checklist:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The <a href="https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/empowering-learners-for-the-age-of-ai_65cd27d4-en.html">OECD and European Commission AI Literacy Framework</a> describes knowledge, skills, and attitudes for primary and secondary education. It is an education framework, not a certification for every AI user.</li>
        <li>The European Commission’s <a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/ai-literacy-questions-answers">AI Act Article 4 Q&amp;A</a> describes measures providers and deployers take to support literacy among staff and other people dealing with systems on their behalf. There is a wording mismatch within the FAQ: its opening summary still says “ensure a sufficient level,” while its later amendment answer says no specific—or “sufficient”—level is mandated for any individual. The amended <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj">Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> and the consolidated <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A02024R1689-20260727">Article 4 text</a> state the updated rule: organizations take measures to support development, consider the staff and other persons’ knowledge, experience, education, training and use context, and consider the people or groups on whom the systems are used. They need not guarantee any specific literacy level for an individual. The FAQ says Article 4 does not require measuring each person’s knowledge or issuing a certificate; training or guidance records are optional examples. Separately, duties concerning staff training for human oversight remain for high-risk AI systems. Read the amended text with the FAQ rather than relying on its unrevised opening summary. This is not legal advice.</li>
        <li>UNESCO’s <a href="https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/recommendation-ethics-artificial-intelligence">Recommendation on the Ethics of AI</a> frames public awareness and literacy through accessible education, civic engagement, human rights, and participation. It is a policy instrument for states and institutions, not a personal conformance scheme.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The Commission also points to the AI Act’s broader rationale: affected people may need enough understanding to recognize how AI-assisted decisions affect them. That public-facing aim is not the same as an Article 4 requirement to measure or certify each affected person.</p>
      <p>Together, these sources support a broader view: literacy includes individual judgment, organizational responsibility, and the conditions that let affected people understand and question systems.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="ec-section">
      <h2>What evidence can and cannot tell us</h2>
      <p>AI reliance is context-sensitive. NIST’s <a href="https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf">Generative AI Profile</a> identifies over-reliance and automation bias as risk considerations in human-AI configuration. It is voluntary risk-management guidance, not proof that every user or task experiences the same effect.</p>
      <p>A 2026 <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-34983-y">Scientific Reports experiment</a> studied 295 participants judging whether faces were real or AI-generated with purported human or AI guidance. Its results concern that experimental task; they do not establish how people rely on AI across professional or high-stakes decisions.</p>
      <p>A 2026 <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S036013152600014X">classroom study</a> of 226 participants found that a critical-thinking intervention reduced direct adoption of AI-generated content in its study setting, while self-reported critical thinking did not significantly improve over the short intervention. That is a useful, bounded result—not proof that reflection alone improves AI literacy generally.</p>
      <p>These findings support care and further inquiry. They do not establish that one reflection routine, verification rule, or interface will work for every person, system, or domain.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="ec-section ec-callout-block">
      <h2>When the stakes rise</h2>
      <p>For decisions that could materially affect health, rights, finances, safety, work, or another person, use AI output as something to examine—not as the only basis for acting.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Trace important claims to sources that are independent of the AI response.</li>
        <li>Check whether the source is current, relevant to the case, and authoritative for the question.</li>
        <li>Bring in qualified human judgment where the decision calls for it, and keep responsibility with the person or institution authorized to decide.</li>
        <li>Record uncertainty, disagreement, and what would change the conclusion.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>These are Hearth &amp; Code’s practical recommendations, not a statement that every law or professional standard imposes the same steps.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="ec-section">
      <h2>A short reflection practice</h2>
      <p>After a consequential or otherwise significant AI interaction, consider:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>What did I rely on, and what source supports it?</li>
        <li>What did I change, reject, or leave uncertain?</li>
        <li>Who could be affected by the result, and who is accountable for the next step?</li>
        <li>What would I check or do differently if I returned to this task?</li>
      </ol>
      <p>This is an optional aid for noticing decisions and uncertainty. It is not a validated assessment, a substitute for domain expertise, or evidence that reflection by itself changes future behavior.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="ec-section ec-counterargument">
      <h2>A limit of individual literacy</h2>
      <p>Asking users to verify more can move too much responsibility onto the people with the least access, time, or power to inspect a system. Literacy cannot replace accountable design, organizational practice, accessible information, public oversight, or meaningful ways to question and refuse a system.</p>
      <p>This page focuses on practical judgment because it is one part of the problem. It does not claim that individual care can resolve structural conditions.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="ec-section">
      <h2>What could change this position</h2>
      <p>This working position should change as better evidence and governance practice emerge. We would revise it if robust evidence showed that another approach better supports understanding and appropriate reliance across different tasks—or if these practices burdened users without improving their ability to question consequential outputs.</p>
    </section>

    <footer class="ec-sources-section">
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ol class="ec-sources-list">
        <li>OECD and European Commission (2026). <em>Empowering Learners for the Age of AI: An AI Literacy Framework for Primary and Secondary Education.</em> <a href="https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/empowering-learners-for-the-age-of-ai_65cd27d4-en.html">OECD publication page</a>.</li>
        <li>European Commission. <em>AI Literacy — Questions &amp; Answers.</em> Article 4 guidance; its opening summary retains earlier “sufficient level” wording, while the amendment answer reflects the updated rule. <a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/ai-literacy-questions-answers">Commission FAQ</a>.</li>
        <li>European Union (2026). <em>Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</em>, amending Article 4 of the AI Act. <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj">EUR-Lex amending regulation</a>.</li>
        <li>European Union (2026). <em>Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, consolidated text as of 27 July 2026</em>, Article 4 as amended. <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A02024R1689-20260727">EUR-Lex consolidated text</a>.</li>
        <li>UNESCO (2021). <em>Recommendation on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence.</em> <a href="https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/recommendation-ethics-artificial-intelligence">UNESCO legal text</a>.</li>
        <li>NIST (2024). <em>Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative Artificial Intelligence Profile</em>, AI 600-1. <a href="https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf">NIST publication</a>.</li>
        <li>Pearson et al. (2026). “Examining human reliance on artificial intelligence in decision making.” <em>Scientific Reports</em>. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-34983-y">doi:10.1038/s41598-026-34983-y</a>.</li>
        <li>Hou et al. (2026). “The effects of critical thinking intervention on reliance behaviors, problem-solving quality, and creativity during human-Generative AI collaborative learning.” <em>Computers &amp; Education</em>. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compedu.2026.105576">doi:10.1016/j.compedu.2026.105576</a>.</li>
      </ol>
    </footer>

    <footer class="ec-revision-section">
      <h2>Revision history</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>2026-09-23</strong>: Earlier page drafted, approved, and published to civics.hearthandcode.dev. This records the prior revision only.</li>
        <li><strong>2026-10-01</strong>: Local review candidate reworks the literacy definition, updates Article 4 context, narrows study claims to their observed tasks, and makes the distinction between personal guidance and legal requirements explicit. This revision also records that the Commission FAQ opening summary retains earlier “sufficient level” wording while its later amendment answer and the amended text state no individual level is required. Exact-copy review and republication remain pending.</li>
      </ul>
    </footer>
  </article>
</main>
</BaseLayout>

